                <pb facs="0267" n="133" sameAs="frus:frus1888p1/pg133"/>

                <head>No. 100.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Rail to Mr. Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States in Central America</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1888-03-16"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >March</hi> 16, 1888</date>. (Received April 7.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 788.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the
                    receipt, this evening, of your instruction No. 554 of the 27th ultimo, relating
                    to the reported disquietude felt in Nicaragua by reason of the rumors that the
                    plan of consolidation attempted by President Barrios in 1885 is to be revived by
                    his successor, President Barillas.</p>
                <p>While I am quite sure that all such rumors are without foundation or
                    justification, I shall take an early opportunity to obtain an authoritative
                    statement in regard to their truth or falsity.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Henry C. Hall.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
